A clear understanding of enzyme classification is essential for interpreting the user’s query. Enzymes are categorized by the reactions they catalyze, and this report focuses on two major classes: oxidoreductases and oxygenases. Oxidoreductases (EC 1.x.x.x) are a broad class of enzymes that facilitate the transfer of electrons from one molecule (the donor) to another (the acceptor). This class includes dehydrogenases and reductases, which are central to various metabolic pathways.6 Oxygenases (EC 1.13.x.x or EC 1.14.x.x), on the other hand, incorporate one or more atoms of molecular oxygen (
O2​
) directly into a substrate molecule. This report will specifically examine a monooxygenase, which incorporates a single oxygen atom.4 The distinction is critical because, while the query terms are similar, they refer to enzymes with fundamentally different catalytic mechanisms and biological functions. For instance, the fungal ERG27 is a reductase involved in biosynthesis, while the bacterial KshAB is a monooxygenase initiating catabolism, a key difference that will be elaborated on in the following sections.

The 5-HT2A receptor is a subtype of the  family, traditionally coupled to  proteins.1 Canonical activation stimulates phospholipase C (PLC), producing inositol triphosphate (
) and diacylglycerol (DAG), which ultimately increases intracellular calcium.1 The 5-HT2A receptor is highly expressed throughout the cortex, making it one of the most extensively studied serotonin receptors.1 It is a critical target for antipsychotic drugs and is central to understanding the mechanism of action of hallucinogenic substances.1 While primarily Gq-coupled, evidence suggests potential G-protein promiscuity, as inhibition of cAMP has been observed, possibly via
activation.1

MSMO1, officially named methylsterol monooxygenase 1, is classified under EC 1.14.13.246 and is also widely recognized by aliases such as SC4MOL, C-4 methylsterol oxidase, and ERG25 (reflecting its yeast ortholog).5
As the oxidative engine of the SC4DMC, MSMO1 catalyzes the first step in the C4-demethylation sequence.2 This function requires molecular oxygen (
) and the reducing agent NADPH.2 The enzyme is tethered to the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) membrane 5 and contains putative metal binding motifs that show similarity to those seen in membrane desaturases and hydroxylases.5 This structural signature suggests that MSMO1 operates using a redox mechanism likely involving an iron ion cofactor to activate oxygen for hydroxylation.5
